<div class="text"> <p align="center"><i>"Have character, but don't be a character." -- <b>Richard Marcinko</b></i></p>
<p>

<ol>
<li>Don't Steal Kills...Ever. It is a violation that is subject to immediate probation in all circumstances where another player reports the violation to a CD officer. These occurrences will be handled on a case-by-case basis and it may result in removing the offending CD member from the guild if the circumstances warrant such action.

If another player KS's a CD member and you think it warrants action by the players Guild, you may report the offense to a CD officer as well as to the offending players Guild. The CD officer will also contact the offending players Guild and locate an officer to discuss the situation and try their best to resolve the situation. At all times and despite the KS event, be as civil as you can with both the offender and any Guild representative you contact.

For the more rare 'camp' monsters, attacking it first is not necessarily entitlement. The person who puts the effort in camping it is the one who should kill it; so don't jump a person's camp.

Be sympathetic to folks and groups who get wiped out from a camp. A bit of bad luck isn't the thumbs-up to muscle in on their 'turf'. Any disputed 'camps' need to be civilly discussed with the other party.<p> 

<li> Don't engage in arguments with other players over /shout or /ooc. This makes the guild look bad and is generally annoying for everyone. If you feel it necessary to discuss differences then take it to /tells.<p>

<li> Don't hog loot! Whenever a Crimson Dragon is in a group you are in fact representing us as a guild. Don't be greedy and allow everyone a fair chance to loot. Be respectful of the loot order that has been decided on by the rest of the group and don't loot out of turn.

Here are some examples to help understand loot rules when there are loot committees involved: <br>
(Loot Committee= LC)
<ol>
<li> CD only raids (1 or 2 groups): Roll or who ever needs and if more than 1 wants, then those roll. Just like your average group when you go off LFGing somewhere. No LC required.<br>

<li> CD only raids (3+ groups): LC by raid leaders or officers present, or Roll, depending on zone and decided by the raid leader who knows what big loot is available in the zone.<br>

<li> CD and Alliance raids (will be usually 3+ groups): Same as above, except the LC will consist of 1 Officer from each guild. 
</ol><p>
Note 1: If no officers are present, then the Raid coordinator shall determine who the LC is for the Guild with no officers present with the consensus of senior members of said Guild. Ideally, the member chosen to be on LC in a raid with no officers present from their guild, should be one of the more senior members, both in rank and time in their respective guild.<p>

Note 2: LC decisions are Final. Each LC member weighs different things like past participation and current items. It is not an exact science, but it's done with the best of intensions as possible. 
<br><br>
<li> Guild say is a way for members to actively participate in asking and answering questions, seeking advice, looking for a group, and having fun when a topic that concerns many people is brought up. 

Basically, guildsay can be used for anything, except the following: auctions, arguments, or having active and long conversations with a single person, because the text can get excessive and be an irritation to others. If you have to talk to a single person, take it to /tells. Never use foul or abusive language on guildsay.<p>

<li> Do not constantly beg for items! This will not be tolerated. If you are at a level where there is an item you should definitely not have, then don't beg other members to get one for you. If you are at a level where that item is within your grasp, then others will be more then happy to help you get it, but we don't actively support excessive tweaking/twinking. That means, for example, do not continually ask members for items for your Monk twink when your Main is an SK.
</ol><p><br>



<h3 align="center">Member Responsibilities</h3>
<ol>
<li> Always be courteous and respectful to your guildmates, Members and Officers alike.<p>

<li> Do your best to attend all guild functions. Guild functions are described as planned Major Raids of appropriate level, Parties, Tournaments, Meetings and other various Events where all guild participation is required if you are on-line. If you are online during the time of a guild event, you should do your best to attend. If no effort is seen on your part to make it there, then action may be taken by guild officers. Of course, RL can preclude your attending, or you're already helping someone else, or you're on a different faction, or you just don't feel in the mood for an event that day. But If, over a period of time, you are *consistantly* in-game elsewhere when everyone else is doing some guild raid/event, then it would be a cause of concern, because one of the most important things about being in a guild is helping each other out.<p>

<li> Check the website regularly! Guild members and leaders often post news and events on the guild forum. You should spend a few minutes at least every other day to browse the website and see what is going on. The forums make up over 80% of our communication. Let's use and abuse it.<p>

<li> Be helpful to other members. If you can provide a service or information that another member needs, you should be willing to do so. Helping each other is what we are here for, and favors to others are always looked on in a good light by other members and officers.<p>

<li> Be a good representative of the guild. This is an open ended responsibility, because all members should know what it means to be a good EQ player. Do your best to be the best.<p>

<li> Be responsive to members logon greetings and farewells. Saying a quick Hi or Bye in /GU really means alot to most of us.<p>

<li> Have FUN!!! This is the most important aspect of the game. The challenge and excitement of the game should always stay fun.<p>
</ol><p><br>




<h3 align="center">Policy on Secondary Characters</h2>
<ol>
<li> There is no limit on the number of Secondary characters you wish to be tagged.<p>

<li> The minimum level Alts can be tagged is Level 9. The reason for level 9 is because there needed to be some cut off level without having a flurry of low level CD alts running around who are only played briefly before they are never seen again. By level 9 it is a more established character and one that will probably be played more often by the person.<p>

<li> New members are required to wait 2 weeks before any alts can be tagged. For anyone who wishes an alt tagged, have your main send a tell to an officer and then log on the alt to be tagged.<p>

<li> If you, as your Main, chooses to leave the guild, and/or is removed from the guild, your alts must be removed as well.<p>

<li> If someone has a Main in another guild, then no secondary characters are allowed in CD. A Main in CD who wishes an Alt to remain in another guild will not normally be allowed, but can be on a case-by-case basis depending on the explanation of the need of the member to the Officers.<br>

Think of CD as a large guild with a small guild view of things: We are a collective of friends who help each other and are committed to each other, beyond loot and epics. It is fundamental in instilling loyalty. An example where Mains and Alts in different guilds can be a negative: Someone is on playing an alt in another guild and a rare mob spawns and we need people, so others playing alts IN CD get the word and log on Mains, but the person playing his alt in another guild has no clue, meaning we have one less person to help out who is actually on-line. Yes, people have friends in the other guilds, but if they wish to take the big step in leaving one guild for another, then we should expect total commitment with Mains and Alts, which still leaves them room for playing with their old guildmates and old friends nonetheless, and at the same time becoming committed friends with the rest of us.<p>
</ol><p><br>


<h3 align="center">Secondary Characters and Loot</h3>


This can be a sticky issue where even reasonable people can disagree. Writing rules for every set of circumstances that may occur ahead of time can be futile as the game can be very complex at times. It is designed that the following guidelines will serve all members.

Loot etiquette:
<ol>
<li> If a person plays two characters at one time on a raid, they are contributing more than one person playing one character.<p>

<li> A person playing two characters may "Aye" on items for each of their characters on a raid/exp group.<p>

<li> Even though a person playing two characters may be contributing more, they are to understand that a single person playing a single character is just as important when it comes to being awarded loot, from an individual, real person at the computer, perspective.<p>

<li> Raids where 'rare' items drop: it is the responsibility of the person playing two characters to understand that if during the raid one of their characters wins a rare item, then passing on a second rare item drop for the second character would be viewed upon favorably, until most others on the raid have been awarded their first "rare" item on that raid, too. Rare items normally being Armor drops like BPs, Arms, Legs, as well as Epic pieces, etc.<p>

<li> Pass downs of Guild Event awarded uber items are expected and the Rule. Pass downs of non-Guild awarded uber items and purchased uber items are not at all expected, but welcomed if one can afford to do so. If a member receives an uber item from another Guildmate, do not sell it...Ever. Continue to pass the item down to another Guildmate who can use it. Thus, once an item that a Guildmate passes down to another Guildmate that was previously won in a non-Guild event or was purchased by the origional owner, it then thereafter becomes a continual Guild passdown item.<p>

<li> Passing down of non-Guild awarded and purchased items to other Guildmates: Some people are very generous and do that out of the goodness of their heart. Passing down items of this type to other members is very much welcomed as a gesture of goodwill and camaraderie among guild members, but will not be enforced due to some people being more poor than others and who will need the older non-guild awarded or purchased items as a means to purchasing/trading for various epic pieces, etc.<p>

<li> Passing down of non-Guild awarded or purchased items to their twinks/alts: Not normally an issue, because there has to be freedom with what people do with non-Guild awarded and purchased items that they can replace. But it is desired that if a member has a non-Guild awarded or purchased uber item that they do Not plan to sell, then giving that item to another members Main rather than their own Alt will benefit the whole guild.<p>
</ol><p><br>



<h3 align="center">Guild Bank</h3>


It is there for all members. Quest gems/items, weps, armor, spells. If you can't sell or pass down an item, please give it to the bank. Members needing items from the bank, don't hesitate to ask as long as it is for your use.<p><br>



<h3 align="center">Arguements Between Members</h3>


Do not fall into the trap of making assumptions about people without being aware of all the facts about any situation. Remember that even if you don't seem to have an easy relationship with someone in the guild, that person has been able to gain membership through successful interactions with others in the guild. All may not share your perceptions. Do not make any personal wars. Civil and effective communication between members who disagree with each other will promote understanding and a truce.<p><br>


<h3 align="center">Officer's Open Door Policy</h3>


Officers of the Crimson Dragons are here for your assistance. Feel free and comfortable to approach any officer at any time with any problem or concern. If the Officer cannot solve the issue, then they WILL find someone who can. No problem is too small or unimportant in seeking advice from the Officers and you will never be turned away. The Crimson Dragon Members are the backbone of the guild and it is the Officers responsibility to make sure any problem or issue is resolved in the best way possible.<p><br>



<h3 align="center">The Code</h3>


The Code is here for direction; it was more vague in the past and that was causing some problems. There are now more specifics. However, some parts of the Code continue to be vague on purpose, because it is hoped that common sense, good judgement and consideration for other Guildmates will be the normal way the Crimson Dragons play with each other and with other Guilds. It is with the understanding that it is up to the person playing the character that they must use good judgment in varying issues, events, raids that come up. If we were to spell out each and every possibility of what to do in each circumstance, no matter how rare or common, it would then be an awfully long Code and boring to read. Use it for direction, use good judgment as best you can, and if in doubt, ask.

Keep in mind that all who play here come to have fun, get well-needed items, and have great adventures as a Team while also tying to keep the Crimson Dragons outstanding reputation at the same time. 

Ideally, keeping an eye on the enjoyment of your fellow adventurer means that in a group or a raid you have many other people keeping an eye out for your fun, too.<p><br>



